n## Steps:\\n1. Extract text from PDF using PyPDF2 to identify table locations\\n2. Create LaTeX files for each table with appropriate formatting\\n3. Use packages: booktabs, multirow, array, makecell, siunitx\\n4. Compile LaTeX files to PDF using pdflatex\\n5. Handle special characters and formatting issues\\n\\n## Table Creation Process:\\n- Table 1: Tool categories with nested lists\\n- Table 2: Dataset benchmarks with multirow cells  \\n- Table 3: Computation costs with numeric formatting using siunitx\\n\\n## Notes:\\n- Install required packages: texlive-latex-base, texlive-latex-extra, texlive-science\\n- For siunitx columns, use {--} for empty numeric cells instead of \"-\"\\n- Handle underfull/overfull warnings by adjusting column widths\\n- Verify output PDFs are generated successfully\\n\\n'}), MCPAction(name='update_operation_guide', arguments={'platform': 'ImageProcessing', 'operation': 'ExtractFiguresFromPDF', 'details': '\\n\\n\\n# Image Processing - Extract Figures from PDF\\n\\n## Steps:\\n1. Use pdftoppm to convert specific PDF pages to images: `pdftoppm -f [start_page] -l [end_page] -png /workspace/survey.pdf /workspace/page[number]`\\n2. Use Python PIL library to crop figures from page images\\n3. Save cropped figures as PDF files\\n4. Verify figure quality and dimensions\\n\\n## Example Commands:\\n- Convert page 3: `pdftoppm -f 3 -l 3 -png /workspace/survey.pdf /workspace/page3`\\n- Convert page 6: `pdftoppm -f 6 -l 6 -png /workspace/survey.pdf /workspace/page6`  \\n- Convert page 7: `pdftoppm -f 7 -l 7 -png /workspace/survey.pdf /workspace/page7`\\n\\n## Python Code:\\n```python\\nfrom PIL import Image\\n\\ndef crop_and_save_figure(page_image_path, figure_id, crop_coords, output_path):\\n    img = Image.open(page_image_path)\\n    cropped = img.crop(crop_coords)  # (left, top, right, bottom)\\n    cropped.save(output_path, \"PDF\", resolution=100.0)\\n```\\n\\n## Notes:\\n- Adjust crop coordinates based on visual inspection of page images\\n- S